Dihydrogen oxide or H2O or water. Now there are three isotopes of 
hydrogen: 1H: one proton in the nucleus, 2H: one proton and one neutron 
in the nucleus, also known as deuterium (D) and 3H: one proton and two 
neutrons in the nucleus, also known as tritium (T).

Now the propotions of H (1H), D (2H) and T (3H) are 99,985%, 0,015% and 
less than 0,0001% (1).

So we can calculate what are the proportions of the following 
compositions of water molecules:

H2O  99,9700022500%      \
HDO   0,0299955000%       \
D2O   0,0000022500%        \
HTO < 0,0001999700% (2)    / (3)
DTO < 0,0000000300%       /
T2O < 0,0000000001%      /

Topi 'Just combinatorics.' Linkala

(1) Typical ocean water proportions for 1H and 2H. 3H decays with 
half-life 12,32 years so it's concentration is quite low even though 
cosmic ray bombarment replenish it in the atmosphere.

(2) This is of course impossible as there is less than 0,0001% of 
tritium to begin with. So the correct way to express this is: HTO < 0,0001%

(3) One should never have so many significant digits in calculations 
like this, but this is just for demonstration purpose, not for real values.
